.IP
.EX
g++ \-DCBOR_STRING_LENGTH_DEFAULT=4096 ...
.EE
.PP
A value above the bound does not compile.
All translation units of one program use the same values: a different
value is a violation of the one definition rule.
.PP
The library does not bound the size of the input.
The program bounds it before it calls the library.
.PP
A limit is checked by the step that reads the item.
\f[CR]lazy::take_ownership\f[R] and \f[CR]lazy::view\f[R] check no
limit.
So a string that is too long gives \f[CR]string_length_exceeded\f[R] at
\f[CR]get\f[R], not when the lazy is made.
.PP
The limits count what a call reads or decodes.
An item that a path or a lazy only skips does not count.
\f[CR]item_size\f[R], \f[CR]decode\f[R] and
\f[CR]diagnostic_notation\f[R] read every item, so every item counts
there.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// owner: a std::shared_ptr<std::string const> with the bytes in the comment above each read\f[R]
\f[I]// built with \-DCBOR_STRING_LENGTH_DEFAULT=2\f[R]
\f[I]// {\(dqa\(dq: \(dqxyz\(dq, \(dqn\(dq: 1}\f[R]
cbor::lazy::view(owner)\->at(\(dqn\(dq)\->get<std::int64_t>();        \f[I]// 1: \(dqxyz\(dq is only skipped\f[R]
cbor::lazy::view(owner)\->at(\(dqa\(dq)\->get<std::string_view>();    \f[I]// string_length_exceeded\f[R]
cbor::item_size(*owner);                                       \f[I]// string_length_exceeded\f[R]
.EE
.SS 5. Nesting depth
The nesting depth counts the arrays, maps and tags around an item.
The top\-level item has the depth 0.
The build sets no limit for it.
A call that recurses gives \f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R] where the
stack of its thread ends: \f[CR]decode\f[R], \f[CR]clone\f[R],
\f[CR]encode\f[R] of an item, \f[CR]diagnostic_notation\f[R], a filter,
a descendant segment, \f[CR]structs\f[R] and \f[CR]schema\f[R].
Each thread reads the bounds of its stack once, at its first check
(../../reference/errors/limits.md).
.PP
An item that a call only skips can be of any depth, and so can the item
of \f[CR]item_size\f[R], the items of \f[CR]sequence_reader\f[R] and a
path of child segments: they do not recurse.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// on a thread with a stack of 64 KiB\f[R]
\f[I]// 65536 nested arrays around 1\f[R]
cbor::diagnostic_notation(bytes);   \f[I]// nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R]
cbor::item_size(bytes);             \f[I]// 65537\f[R]
.EE
.SS 6. Memory
The caller sets the bound of the memory of a decode.
\f[CR]structs<T>::decode\f[R] and \f[CR]schema<T>::decode\f[R] take a
target as their third argument.
The target holds \f[CR]std::pmr\f[R] containers with a
\f[CR]std::pmr::memory_resource\f[R].
The decode reads into the target and makes each element with the
allocator of its container.
When the resource refuses, the decode returns
\f[CR]error::not_enough_memory\f[R].
A \f[CR]std::bad_alloc\f[R] does not leave \f[CR]structs<T>::decode\f[R]
or \f[CR]schema<T>::decode\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]using\f[R] texts = std::pmr::vector<std::pmr::string>;
\f[B]auto\f[R] const owner = std::make_shared<std::string const>(
    *cbor::structs<std::vector<std::string>>::encode({std::string(40, \(aqa\(aq), std::string(40, \(aqb\(aq)}));
std::array<std::byte, 64> buffer{};
std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource budget(buffer.data(), buffer.size(), std::pmr::null_memory_resource());
cbor::structs<texts>::decode(owner, texts(&budget));   \f[I]// not_enough_memory\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The library takes no allocator in other places.
These places allocate with \f[CR]operator new\f[R], through the standard
containers and \f[CR]std::make_shared\f[R].
A failed allocation there throws \f[CR]std::bad_alloc\f[R].
A plain struct inside a \f[CR]std::pmr\f[R] container gets no allocator,
so its members use the default resource.
.PP
