.SS 7. Threads
The library starts no thread and holds no lock.
It has no global that changes: the limits are constants.
.PP
A call over bytes, for example \f[CR]at_path<Path, T>(encoded)\f[R] and
\f[CR]item_size\f[R], has no shared state.
Two lazy values from two calls of \f[CR]lazy::take_ownership\f[R] or
\f[CR]lazy::view\f[R] share nothing, also over the same bytes.
.PP
A lazy holds only its owner, its bytes and its offset, and a read
changes nothing in it.
So several threads read one lazy, and the lazy values of one top\-level
item, at the same time.
\f[CR]lazy::take_ownership\f[R] gives a \f[CR]std::expected\f[R], and
\f[CR]std::move(*l)\f[R] moves the lazy out of it:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// [1, 2]\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] l = cbor::lazy::take_ownership(std::move(bytes));
std::thread([](cbor::lazy const &v) {
    v.at(1)\->get<std::int64_t>();   \f[I]// 2\f[R]
}, std::move(*l)).join();
.EE
.SS 8. Owners
A \f[CR]cbor::lazy\f[R] holds the owner of the bytes.
So does every lazy that a step gives, and every iterator.
A lazy from \f[CR]at\f[R] stays valid after the lazy that gave it and
after the \f[CR]std::string\f[R] that went into
\f[CR]lazy::take_ownership\f[R] end.
.IP
.EX
std::optional<cbor::lazy> keep;
{
    std::string bytes = message();   \f[I]// {\(dqtags\(dq: [\(dqa\(dq, \(dqb\(dq]}\f[R]
    keep = *cbor::lazy::take_ownership(std::move(bytes))\->at(\(dqtags\(dq);
}
keep\->at(1)\->get<std::string_view>();   \f[I]// \(dqb\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A text or a byte string comes back in a \f[CR]cbor::ref<T>\f[R].
The \f[CR]ref\f[R] holds the owner of the bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]*r\f[R] gives the value.
A view comes back by value, any other \f[CR]T\f[R] as
\f[CR]T const &\f[R].
\f[CR]r\->\f[R] reaches the members of the value.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]*\f[R] and \f[CR]\->\f[R] do not compile on a temporary
\f[CR]ref\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]==\f[R] compares the value with a \f[CR]T\f[R] or with another
\f[CR]ref\f[R], as \f[CR]std::optional\f[R] does.
\f[CR]==\f[R] takes a temporary, because it gives a \f[CR]bool\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
A copy of a \f[CR]ref\f[R] shares the owner.
It does not copy the bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]r.clone()\f[R] copies the value out: a \f[CR]std::string\f[R] for
a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R], a \f[CR]std::vector<std::byte>\f[R] for a
\f[CR]std::span<std::byte const>\f[R], and a \f[CR]cbor::owning<T>\f[R]
for any other \f[CR]T\f[R].
.PP
The owner of the bytes comes from one of these:
.PP
