.PP
A \f[CR]std::string\f[R] lvalue and a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] alone
do not compile, because no form copies the bytes:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]auto\f[R] const shared = std::make_shared<std::string const>(message());
\f[B]auto\f[R] const a = cbor::lazy::view(shared).value();   \f[I]// shares the bytes, no copy\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const b = cbor::lazy::take_ownership(std::move(bytes)).value();   \f[I]// moves the string, no copy\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]structs<T>::decode\f[R], \f[CR]schema<T>::decode\f[R],
\f[CR]schema<T>::path\f[R] and \f[CR]at_path\f[R] for a view result
(path.md) take the same forms.
\f[CR]cbor::sequence\f[R] takes the two forms with a
\f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R], and no rvalue \f[CR]std::string\f[R].
The library takes no other pointer to bytes.
.PP
A larger object can hold the bytes.
Two forms read them in place.
The aliasing constructor of \f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R] makes an owner of
the string from the object.
Or the object is the owner, and a view of its bytes goes beside it.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] page {
    std::string bytes;   \f[I]// \(dqxyz\(dq\f[R]
};
\f[B]auto\f[R] const p = std::make_shared<page const>(message());
std::shared_ptr<std::string const> const owner(p, &p\->bytes);   \f[I]// shares the count of p\f[R]
cbor::lazy::view(owner)\->get<std::string_view>();               \f[I]// \(dqxyz\(dq, a view into p\->bytes\f[R]
cbor::lazy::view(p, std::string_view(p\->bytes))\->get<std::string_view>();   \f[I]// the same view\f[R]
.EE
.PP
An empty owner throws \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
A \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] beside an owner compiles, and the library
does not check that the owner holds its bytes.
A \f[CR]std::string\f[R] beside an owner does not compile, because it is
not a borrowed range: its bytes can end before the owner ends.
